INT. TRAIN STATION - LOST AND FOUND OFFICE - EVENING

A narrow room of wire shelves heaped with umbrellas, scarves, odd gloves, a taxidermied owl in a glass bell jar. A brass counter bell. A flickering fluorescent strip.

HELENA MARCZAK (50s, cardigan, lanyard) sits behind the counter with a ledger. She writes slowly, ignoring the clock above her.

A MAN enters. Mid-forties, gray overcoat, wet hair. He holds a ticket stub like a talisman and does not step past the mat.

MAN
Hello. I lost something.

HELENA
Everyone has.

MAN
A leather satchel. Brown. It has my initials.

HELENA
Which are?

MAN
T.R.

HELENA
That's not much.

MAN
It is to me.

Helena turns pages. Her finger travels down a column. She stops.

HELENA
Brown satchel, T.R. Brought in Tuesday. Found on Platform Four.

MAN
Yes.

HELENA
Under a bench.

MAN
I was waiting for a train.

HELENA
Which one?

The man hesitates.

MAN
I don't remember.

Helena sets down her pen, looks at him properly. His hands shake slightly.

HELENA
Sir, what's your name?

MAN
Teodor.

HELENA
Teodor what?

TEODOR
Rusu.

HELENA
Then it's yours, Mr. Rusu.

She goes to the shelf, lifts the satchel, sets it on the counter between them. Teodor stares at it but does not touch it.

TEODOR
What's in it?

HELENA
I don't open them.

TEODOR
Would you?

HELENA
That's not my job.

Teodor unbuckles the strap. His hand freezes at the flap. He looks up.

TEODOR
^I'm afraid.

HELENA
^I know.

They say it together, softly, and the fluorescent strip buzzes. Teodor opens the flap. Inside, a child's red mitten.

He lifts it out. Holds it against his chest.

TEODOR (CONT'D)
(barely audible)
Thank you.

Helena nods. Writes nothing in the ledger. Behind her the owl watches from its jar.

TEODOR (CONT'D)
Do you keep things that no one comes for?

HELENA
Seven years. Then we donate them.

TEODOR
Seven years.

HELENA
Mr. Rusu. You came.

He nods, once. Tucks the mitten into his coat. Walks out into the rain.
